  "live_records": {
    "q-162755": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162755",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{3}x+12y\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=15.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=15.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=15\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2\\sqrt{3},12⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}=2\\lambda x \\\\ 12=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=15$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =15 \\\\ \\frac{3+12}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =15 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt{3},\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\sqrt{3},-2),\\,(\\sqrt{3},2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\sqrt{3},-2)\\,$ | $\\,(\\sqrt{3},2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-30\\,$ | $\\,30\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\sqrt{3},-2),$ and its minimum value is $-30.$",
