    "q-37869": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37869",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+3y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=13\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=13$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,-3),\\,(2,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,-3)$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-13$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,3),$ and its maximum value is $13.$",
