    "q-99037": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99037",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=25-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=\\lambda (2y-4)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1+\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1+\\lambda )=2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0,$ the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,4).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1,$ from the second equation of the system we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-1)=2(-1)\\,⇒\\,0=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $25$ | $9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $25.$",
