    "q-101317": {
      "dom_id": "step-q101317",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3y+3z$ on the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=48.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=48.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,3,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=4\\lambda y \\\\ 3=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{4\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{8\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=48$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ {0}^{2}+2(\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}})+4(\\frac{9}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =48 \\\\ \\frac{9}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{18}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{27}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{256} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=\\pm 4,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-4,-2),\\,(0,4,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-4,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(0,4,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-18\\,$ | $\\,18\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,4,2),$ and its maximum value is $18.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
